<h1>SHA-1 vs MD5 vs SHA-256: A Practical Comparison</h1>
<p>MD5, SHA-1, and SHA-256 often appear together in old tutorials, checksum lists, and programming examples. They are all hash functions, but they are not equally safe choices. The easiest practical comparison is this: MD5 and SHA-1 are legacy algorithms with known weaknesses, while SHA-256 remains a common modern default for general-purpose hashing and file integrity.</p>
<p>MD5 produces a 128-bit digest and was published as <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1321.html">RFC 1321</a>. It is fast and widely available, but collision attacks make it unsuitable for serious cryptographic protection. SHA-1 produces a 160-bit digest and was once a major standard, but it also has a long history of weakening security confidence.</p>
<p>NIST has been clear about SHA-1. In its 2022 announcement on <a href="https://www.nist.gov/news-events/news/2022/12/nist-transitioning-away-sha-1-all-applications">transitioning away from SHA-1</a>, NIST said it would transition away from SHA-1 for cryptographic protection by December 31, 2030. That does not mean every old SHA-1 value disappears, but it does show the direction for new systems.</p>
<p>SHA-256 is part of the SHA-2 family and is specified in <a href="https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/fips/180-4/upd1/final">FIPS 180-4</a>. It outputs 256 bits and is supported almost everywhere. For publishing file checksums, identifying build artifacts, and replacing older MD5 or SHA-1 checksums, SHA-256 is usually the practical answer.</p>
<p>For legacy work, you may still need MD5 or SHA-1. A vendor may publish only an MD5 checksum. A Git repository may expose SHA-1 object identifiers depending on its configuration. An old database may contain MD5 hashes. In these cases, the right response is not panic. The right response is to understand the context and avoid expanding the legacy pattern into new security decisions.</p>
<p>For passwords, the comparison changes again. None of these three should be used as a plain password hash. Password hashing needs salts and work factors, not only a longer digest. If a team is replacing MD5 or SHA-1 in a login database, switching to plain SHA-256 is still not enough because it remains fast to calculate.</p>
<p>A useful September maintenance task is to inventory where these algorithms appear in your projects. Search documentation, CI scripts, database fields, download pages, and internal tools. If MD5 or SHA-1 appears only for non-security compatibility, document the reason. If either appears in password storage, signing, certificates, or tamper-resistant workflows, plan a migration.</p>
<p>For documentation, be explicit about why a legacy algorithm remains present. A note such as MD5 kept only for compatibility is better than leaving future developers to guess. Good comments prevent accidental reuse. They also make audits faster because reviewers can separate known legacy constraints from places where weak hashing was copied into new code.</p>
<p>When replacing algorithms, update tests as well as code. Old unit tests may contain fixed MD5 or SHA-1 values. Replace them with SHA-256 examples and clearly name the expected algorithm. Otherwise, future contributors may see the old test fixture and assume the old algorithm is still approved.</p>
<p>The final rule is simple. Use MD5 only for compatibility and basic non-security checks. Treat SHA-1 as retired for new cryptographic protection. Use SHA-256 for new general-purpose hashing and checksums. Use a dedicated password hashing algorithm for passwords. That split keeps each tool in the role it can still handle responsibly.</p>
